/*
 * research/metasmith_benchmark/drivers/e1_nfcore/fir.config -- nf-core/mag 5.5.0 Slurm execution profile for the
 * DRAC "fir" cluster.
 *
 * Modeled on the metasmith engine's own Slurm preset (src/metasmith/nextflow_config/slurm.nf):
 * one Slurm job per task (--nodes=1 --ntasks=1), an account flag, a bounded submission queue,
 * and reliance on the pipeline's own retry policy rather than re-deriving one. nf-core/mag's
 * conf/base.config already carries realistic per-process cpu/memory/time -- via the
 * process_medium/process_high labels and named overrides for MEGAHIT, FLYE, METABAT2_METABAT2,
 * GTDBTK_CLASSIFYWF etc. -- tuned for real assemblies, not the test profile's toy numbers, and
 * its top-level errorStrategy (retry on exit codes 130-145, 104, 175-177) already covers every
 * Slurm kill signal a preempted or OOM-killed task can exit with (137 SIGKILL, 139 SIGSEGV,
 * 143 SIGTERM, 134 SIGABRT are all inside 130-145). base.config loads unconditionally, before
 * any profile, so this file must never redeclare cpus/memory/time/errorStrategy at the same
 * selector -- only add the Slurm-specific directives on top.
 *
 * fir has no network on compute nodes (confirmed: they move under 1 MB/s to any external
 * host). This profile assumes every container image nf-core/mag 5.5.0 needs is already
 * converted to a cached .img under apptainer.cacheDir before a single task submits -- done
 * from a login node, see prepull.sh alongside this file. A task that has to pull on a compute
 * node hangs rather than fails cleanly, so an incomplete cache is a silent-hang risk, not a
 * fast error. apptainer.cacheDir is set structurally below (not left to an exported
 * NXF_APPTAINER_CACHEDIR) so that invariant holds for any launch of this profile, not only one
 * that happens to export the same environment run_test.sh does.
 *
 * fir exposes wall-time BANDS as separate partitions (measured via `sinfo -o "%P %l %c %m"`):
 * cpubase_bynode_b1..b6 span 3h/12h/1d/3d/7d/28d on the same 192-core/768GB nodes. The first
 * version of this file picked a band explicitly via a `queue` closure keyed on task.time --
 * that is WRONG, verified by running it: sbatch rejected every task with "The specified
 * partition does not exist, or the submitted job cannot fit in it ... simply submit the job
 * without the --partition option, the scheduler will redirect it to the most suitable partition
 * automatically" (out_test1b, FASTQC_RAW). fir's submit filter does the band routing itself from
 * the job's requested `--time`; naming one yourself loses, every time. So: never set `queue` /
 * `--partition` here. A task whose base.config `time` needs a band above b1 (FLYE, METASPADES on
 * retry, GTDBTK_CLASSIFYWF) is still expected to auto-route correctly, but that part is
 * unverified -- every task actually run so far finished within the b1 band's 3h.
 *
 * A second, unrelated blocker showed up launching the DRIVER itself (not a Slurm task): the
 * login node's shared per-user cgroup (see the campaign's own pids.max=512 finding) starves the
 * JVM of threads before it even reaches Nextflow's own startup code -- `GC(0) Failed to create
 * worker thread` / `unable to create native thread`, seconds in, well before any task submits.
 * The JVM sizes its own GC and common-fork-join thread pools off `nproc` (64 on this login node),
 * so on a shared, contended login node it can lose a burst-of-dozens-of-threads race at boot even
 * when the account is nowhere near 512 total. Passing `-XX:ActiveProcessorCount=4` in NXF_OPTS
 * (alongside `-Xmx6g`) fixed it outright here -- verified: five consecutive launches died at JVM
 * boot without it, the first launch with it reached real Slurm submission and ran to completion.
 * Carry this in every driver invocation on fir, not just this profile's own validation runs.
 *
 * Taxonomy (GTDB-Tk): DISABLED outright (skip_gtdbtk = true, control.config), not routed to
 * the pre-staged tree. A bare `gtdb_db` default is a live 110 GB URL (nextflow.config:108) and
 * workflows/mag.nf resolves whatever --gtdb_db points at with `file(..., checkIfExists: true)`
 * on the driver itself (a login node) before any task submits -- so an unset gtdb_db does not
 * fail closed, it starts a 110 GB fetch from fir's login node the moment the run starts, and
 * skip_gtdbtk defaults false, so this fires unless something sets one of the two.
 *
 * A full GTDB release232 reference package IS already staged at
 * /scratch/phyberos/staging/gtdb/release232 (15 GB: taxonomy, markers, msa, masks, mrca_red,
 * pplacer, radii, split, metadata), and subworkflows/local/gtdbtk/main.nf's
 * `val_gtdb.isDirectory()` branch would consume it directly, skipping both the network fetch
 * and GTDBTK_DB_PREPARATION's untar task. That was this file's first answer -- reversed once
 * the campaign lead confirmed the metasmith side of this comparison is also dropping GTDB-Tk
 * classification from what it scores. Pointing this arm at a real reference to produce a
 * taxonomy nothing compares against is pure cost for no comparison, cheaper to disable than to
 * route through a local file just because the file happens to be free. skip_binqc's own OR
 * (`gtdb = params.skip_binqc || params.skip_gtdbtk ? false : params.gtdb_db`) means
 * skip_gtdbtk alone is sufficient -- gtdb_db is never referenced, let alone resolved, once it
 * is set.
 *
 * CheckM2's database (checkm2_db) is a `file-path` in the schema pointing at the .dmnd file
 * itself, not a directory (nextflow_schema.json's bin_quality_check_options.checkm2_db:
 * "Path to local file of an already downloaded and uncompressed CheckM2 database file (.dmnd
 * file)."). The tarball fetched by prepull.sh's companion checkm2_db step is now extracted at
 * /scratch/phyberos/wave2_b3_nfcore/checkm2_db/CheckM2_database/ -- point at the .dmnd file
 * inside it, not the enclosing directory and not the .tar.gz.
 */
profiles {
    fir {
        apptainer {
            enabled     = true
            autoMounts  = true
            pullTimeout = "60m"
            cacheDir    = "/scratch/phyberos/wave2_b3_nfcore/apptainer_cache/nxf"
        }

        params {
            // gtdb_db deliberately NOT set -- taxonomy is disabled outright (skip_gtdbtk =
            // true, control.config), so this would be staged and unused. See the header
            // comment for why disabling won over pointing at the already-staged tree.
            checkm2_db = "/scratch/phyberos/wave2_b3_nfcore/checkm2_db/CheckM2_database/uniref100.KO.1.dmnd"
        }

        process {
            executor       = "slurm"
            // Lazy (closure) rather than an interpolated string: a plain GString field is
            // evaluated once at config-parse time, and an override supplied on the same command
            // line as this file races that evaluation against param assignment. A closure defers
            // evaluation to per-task submission, after all params are resolved, which is the
            // only form that is safe regardless of --slurm_account's position on the line.
            // The exclude list matches FIR_BAD_NODES in drivers/_common.py: nodes whose Lustre client failed R1 tasks.
            clusterOptions = { "--nodes=1 --ntasks=1 --account=${params.slurm_account ?: 'rrg-shallam-ab'} --exclude=fc30372,fc30557,fc30559,fc30560,fc30564,fc30567,fc30568,fc30570,fc30604,fc30608,fc30609,fc30622,fc30623,fc30628,fc30640" }
            // SLURM_TMPDIR is only set inside a Slurm job step; fall through to TMPDIR/tmp so a
            // stray local-executor task (or a future withLabel escape hatch) doesn't scratch to
            // an unset variable. Mirrors src/metasmith/nextflow_config/slurm.nf's own default
            // chain rather than the bare form this file shipped with.
            scratch        = '${SLURM_TMPDIR:-${TMPDIR:-/tmp}}'
            // No job arrays. With array = 100, nextflow submits a partial batch only once its
            // upstream closes, so one element whose failure nextflow never registers holds every
            // later batch: E1 short sat idle from 21:18 on 2026-09-14 behind SemiBin2 element
            // 59892107_98, FAILED in Slurm and SUBMITTED in nextflow. Single submissions pay
            // fir's 10/min submitRateLimit, which the remaining per-sample stages can afford.
            array          = 0
            // No `queue`/`--partition` here -- see the header comment. fir's own submit filter
            // routes each job to the right wall-time band from `--time` alone.

            // No withLabel: 'xlocalx' here (removed this round): that label is metasmith's own
            // convention and matches nothing nf-core/mag 5.5.0 ever declares, so the block was
            // silently inert -- every process, network-needing or not, was already going to a
            // compute node regardless of this block's presence. Nothing in the pinned control
            // config needs network: checkm2_db is pre-staged as above and GTDB-Tk (the other
            // network-needing default) is disabled outright. If a future change adds a step
            // that must reach the network, target its real process name with `withName:`, not
            // a label the pipeline never emits.
        }

        executor {
            queueSize       = 100
            submitRateLimit = "10/1min"
            pollInterval    = "30sec"
        }
    }
}
